YUME for Debian
===============

The yume package installs the client binary (`yume`), documentation, and
examples. The server daemon (`yumed`), `/etc/yume`, and the disabled systemd
unit are shipped separately in the yume-daemon package.

It does not auto-start `yumed`. A public server needs operator-provided TLS
certificates, `authorized_keys`, optional `auth_keys.meta`, optional PQ key
material, and any facade/obfuscation settings before it should listen on the
network.

Example configuration files are installed under:

    /usr/share/doc/yume/examples/

For a first server setup, install yume-daemon, create TLS material and
`/etc/yume/authorized_keys`, add at least one client public key, then run
`yumed` explicitly with either `/etc/yume/yumed.json` or CLI flags. See
`man yumed`, `man yume`, and `/usr/share/doc/yume/QUICKSTART.md` for the
current command examples.

Binding to TCP port 443 requires root or `cap_net_bind_service`. Avoid putting
YUME behind an HTTP-mode TLS terminator; use TCP passthrough when a proxy or
load balancer sits in front of `yumed`.
